      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Ask for "paver sand" at a supply yard and you may be handed either of two materials, depending on what the person behind the counter assumes you are doing. Knowing which one you actually need — and being able to say so in the right words — is most of what this page is for.
        </p>

        <h2 id="definition" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What Paver Sand Actually Is
        </h2>

        <p>
          Paver sand is a general trade term for the coarse, angular, washed sands used to build and finish an interlocking paver surface. It is not a single graded product with one specification behind it, which is precisely why the term causes so much confusion. In an installation, sand appears twice, in two roles that place opposite demands on the material.
        </p>

        <p>
          Underneath the pavers there is a thin <strong>bedding layer</strong>, usually about an inch thick, screeded flat over a compacted gravel base. Its job is to give each paver a uniform seat and to let the units settle a few millimetres into place under compaction so they end up level with each other. That sand must be coarse, angular, free-draining, and stable under load.
        </p>

        <p>
          Between the pavers there is <strong>joint sand</strong>, swept into the gaps once the pavers are laid and vibrated in. Its job is completely different: it creates the interlock that makes a paver field behave as a single flexible surface rather than a collection of loose blocks, and it keeps water, weeds and insects out of the joints. It needs to be fine enough to fill a narrow gap fully, and to stay there.
        </p>

        <p>
          One coarse material for structure, one fine material for interlock. Almost every problem people describe with paver sand comes from using the wrong one in the wrong place — or, most commonly, from using an ordinary sand that is unsuited to either.
        </p>

        <h2 id="two-jobs" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Bedding Sand vs Joint Sand: The Two Different Jobs
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Bedding Sand</h3>
        <p>
          The correct material is <strong>washed concrete sand</strong>, sometimes sold as bedding sand, C33 sand, or simply "concrete sand". It is coarse — grains up to a few millimetres — with sharply angular particles produced by crushing rather than the rounding that comes from water transport. Washed means the fines have been rinsed out, so it drains freely instead of holding water against the underside of the pavers.
        </p>
        <p>
          The bedding layer is <strong>one inch, screeded, and never compacted before the pavers go down</strong>. This is the detail most DIY installations get wrong. A compacted bedding layer gives the pavers nowhere to settle into, and a layer thicker than an inch behaves like a cushion: it moves under load, and the surface develops ruts and dips within a season or two. The base carries the load; the sand only levels.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Joint Sand</h3>
        <p>
          Joint sand is swept across the finished surface, worked into the gaps with a broom, and then vibrated in with a plate compactor so it packs down and the joints can be topped up. Historically this was fine silica sand or a finer grade of the same concrete sand. Today, most installations use <strong>polymeric sand</strong> — graded sand with binders blended through it that activate when the surface is watered and cure to lock the grains together.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reason for the shift is straightforward. Plain sand in a joint washes out in heavy rain, gets blown out by leaf blowers, provides a perfect seedbed for weeds, and is easily excavated by ants. Polymeric sand resists all four. It is not maintenance-free — it can crack, it needs a proper watering-in, and it has its own failure modes covered in our guide to <a href="/polymeric-sand-installation-mistakes/">polymeric sand installation mistakes</a> — but it is a substantial improvement over loose sand in a joint.
        </p>

        <!-- Comparison Table -->
