        <h2 id="what-makes-it-work" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Why Angular Grains Matter More Than Anything Else
        </h2>

        <p>
          The single property that separates a sand suitable for pavers from one that is not is grain shape. Crushed sands have sharp, irregular faces; when compacted, the particles wedge against each other and the mass develops genuine shear strength — it resists being pushed sideways. Sands that have been tumbled by water or wind, such as beach sand and play sand, have rounded grains that behave like ball bearings: apply load and they roll past each other, and the layer redistributes rather than holding shape.
        </p>

        <p>
          Gradation matters almost as much. A well-graded sand contains a spread of particle sizes so the smaller grains fill the voids between the larger ones and the packed density is high. A uniformly sized sand leaves large voids no matter how much you compact it, which is why an apparently "clean-looking" fine sand can settle noticeably in the first winter.
        </p>

        <p>
          The third factor is fines content. Silt and clay particles hold water, and water in a bedding layer is what makes it soft in spring and pushes pavers upward when it freezes. That is what the "washed" in washed concrete sand refers to — those fines have been rinsed out deliberately, and it is worth paying for.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1"</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Bedding sand depth — no more</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4-6"</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Compacted gravel base, patio</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-12"</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Compacted base, driveway</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">1.3 t</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Weight of a cubic yard of sand</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="which-sand" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The Sands You Will Actually Be Offered
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Washed Concrete Sand (Bedding Sand, C33)</h3>
        <p>
          The correct bedding material and the one paver manufacturers specify. Coarse, angular, washed, free-draining. Sold loose by the cubic yard at any aggregate supplier, and in bags at a considerable price premium. For anything larger than a small patio, buying loose is far cheaper even after delivery.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Polymeric Sand</h3>
        <p>
          Joint sand with binders that cure when watered. It resists washout, weeds and ants, and produces a joint that stays where you put it. It is more expensive per square foot than plain sand, it must be applied to a completely dry surface, and it must be watered in exactly as the bag directs. Our <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">polymeric sand reviews</a> cover which brands hold up, and <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">how to use polymeric sand</a> covers the method.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Fine Silica or Mason Sand for Joints</h3>
        <p>
          The traditional joint filler, still used on permeable installations and by installers who prefer a joint that can be topped up rather than one that sets. It is cheap and it never cracks, because there is nothing in it to crack — but it washes out, grows weeds, and hosts ants. Reasonable for a low-traffic path where you do not mind sweeping more sand in each spring, and covered in more depth in our <a href="/polymeric-sand-alternatives/">polymeric sand alternatives</a> guide.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Stone Dust and Screenings</h3>
        <p>
          The fine waste from crushing stone. It compacts extremely hard, which some installers like for bedding, but it also holds water and is prone to frost heave in cold climates. Most paver manufacturers specify washed concrete sand rather than screenings, and if the pavers carry a warranty it is worth following that specification.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-not-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. What Not to Use
        </h2>

        <p>
          Three sands come up constantly, all of them cheap, all of them available at the same store as the pavers, and none of them suitable.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Play sand</strong> is washed, screened and rounded specifically so it is soft and safe for children — every property that makes it good in a sandbox makes it wrong under pavers. It will not lock up, it stays soft under load, and a patio bedded on it will be visibly uneven within a season. We take the comparison apart in <a href="/paver-sand-vs-play-sand/">paver sand vs play sand</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Mason sand</strong> is fine and uniform, blended to make mortar workable rather than to compact under load. In joints it is passable in the same way plain silica sand is; as bedding it is too fine, holds water, and gives the same settling problems as play sand.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Beach or river sand</strong> is rounded by definition, often carries salt or silt, and behaves worse than either of the above. It is also, in many places, illegal to remove in quantity.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. How Much Paver Sand You Need
        </h2>

        <p>
          For bedding, a one-inch layer over a given area takes roughly <strong>one cubic yard per 300 square feet</strong>. A cubic yard of damp sand weighs around 1.3 tons, so a modest 400-square-foot patio needs about a yard and a half — nearly two tons of material, which is a delivery rather than a car trip. Add ten per cent for the screed waste, the low spots in your base, and the pile you will inevitably lose into the grass.
        </p>

        <p>
          For joints, the coverage depends on joint width and paver thickness, which is why bag figures vary so widely. As a rough guide, narrow joints on standard pavers take about one 50-pound bag per 50 to 100 square feet; wide joints or flagstone with irregular gaps can use two or three times that. Buy one bag more than the calculation suggests — running out mid-job means the joints cure at different times and often look different afterwards.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our <a href="/sand-calculator/">paver sand calculator</a> does both figures from your dimensions, including the base depth, which is the part most people underestimate.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Bagged or Loose?</h3>
        <p>
          Bagged bedding sand is convenient and expensive. At typical yard prices, a cubic yard delivered loose costs a fraction of what the same volume costs in bags, and the crossover point comes surprisingly early — somewhere around a hundred square feet of patio. Loose sand also arrives damp, which is an advantage: damp sand screeds cleanly and does not blow around, whereas bone-dry bagged sand runs off the screed board and takes longer to level.
        </p>
        <p>
          The one argument for bags is storage. A yard of sand tipped on a driveway has to be used or moved before it rains and before the neighbours object; bags can sit in a garage until the weekend the base is finally ready. On a small project spread over several weekends, that flexibility can be worth the premium.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Paver Sand Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Sand as the Base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sand is a levelling layer, not a structural one. Without four to six inches of compacted gravel under it — eight to twelve for a driveway — the surface will settle no matter how carefully the sand was screeded. This single error accounts for most sunken patios.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Making the Bedding Layer Too Thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One inch, screeded. Two or three inches of sand used to bridge an uneven base behaves like a cushion, moves under load, and produces ruts. Fix the base with base material, not with extra sand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Compacting the Bedding Sand Before Laying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The pavers need to settle a few millimetres into the screeded sand when the plate compactor passes over them. Compacting the sand first removes that allowance and leaves the surface uneven, with individual units standing proud.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Play Sand Because It Was Cheaper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rounded grains cannot interlock. Play sand under pavers stays soft, redistributes under load, and produces a wavy surface within a season — and relaying the patio costs far more than the sand ever saved.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sweeping Polymeric Sand Into Damp Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The binders activate on contact with water. Applied to joints that are still damp from rain or washing, the sand sets partway down, leaves voids underneath, and hazes the paver faces. The surface has to be genuinely dry before the bag is opened.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is paver sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A coarse, angular, washed sand used in two roles: a one-inch bedding layer the pavers are set into, and joint sand swept into the gaps afterwards. The two jobs need different materials — coarse concrete sand for bedding, and a finer, usually polymeric, sand for the joints.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of sand do you use under pavers?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Washed concrete sand, also sold as bedding sand or ASTM C33 sand. Coarse, angular and free-draining, screeded to about one inch over a compacted gravel base — and never deeper, because a thick sand layer ruts and settles under load.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is paver sand the same as regular sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. Play sand and mason sand are fine and rounded, so the grains roll past each other instead of interlocking and the layer never stabilises. Paver sand is deliberately coarse and angular so the grains wedge together under compaction.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much paver sand do I need?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">For bedding, roughly one cubic yard per 300 square feet at one inch deep — about 1.3 tons per yard. For joints, about one 50-pound bag per 50 to 100 square feet on narrow joints, and considerably more for wide joints or flagstone.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use the same sand for bedding and joints?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Coarse concrete sand can be swept into joints and was standard practice for decades, but it washes out, grows weeds and hosts ants. Modern practice uses a dedicated joint sand — usually polymeric — and keeps the coarse sand for the bedding layer, where its angularity does the real work.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need gravel under paver sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Four to six inches of compacted crushed gravel for a patio, eight to twelve for a driveway, compacted in lifts. Sand laid straight onto soil is the most common reason a patio sinks within a couple of seasons.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between paver sand and polymeric sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Polymeric sand is joint sand with binders that activate when watered and cure to hold the grains together against washout, weeds and ants. Plain paver sand has no binder. Polymeric sand goes only in the joints — never as bedding, where a hardened layer would defeat the flexible, free-draining behaviour the system depends on.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use stone dust instead of paver sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Some installers bed on stone dust because it compacts very hard, but it holds water and is prone to frost heave in cold climates. Most paver manufacturers specify washed concrete sand for bedding, and following that specification matters if the pavers carry a warranty.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

