        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Paver sand</strong> is two materials, not one. Washed concrete sand goes under the pavers, one inch deep, screeded and not pre-compacted. A dedicated joint sand — polymeric for most installations — goes between them, vibrated in and topped up. Get those two right and the sand will never be the reason the patio fails.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            And remember what the sand is not: it is not the base. The gravel underneath carries the load, and no grade of sand compensates for skimping on it. Build the base properly, use angular washed sand at the correct depth, and fill the joints with something designed to stay in them — that is the whole specification.
          </p>
